{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_017zBCN8ZibnFynqNtNfxv1f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NLwZVY4HMug3z84zzfQVNs","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_201/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_201/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 117,\n    \"comment\": \"Draw-time miracle never fires in real gameplay. The static grant (miracle_cost / apply_miracle_grant) only stamps cards that are ALREADY in the controller's hand (it checks game.get_hand(controller).contains(card)). But engine/game.draw_card reads get_miracle_cost off the card it just drew off the LIBRARY, which was never stamped. So in a real game the just-drawn instant/sorcery has no miracle stamp and offer_miracle is a no-op. The end-to-end behavior only passes in tests because TestLoreholdMiracleDrawCast._build pre-stamps the top library card manually (tests.py lines 455-457, with a comment acknowledging this). Per Oracle text the engine must offer miracle on the natural first draw of any instant/sorcery the controller draws while Lorehold is in play. The grant needs to apply to cards in the library top / as-drawn (e.g. stamp on draw, or have the draw-time hook consult Lorehold's grant), not only cards resting in hand.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"engine/miracle.py\",\n    \"line\": 60,\n    \"comment\": \"The miracle stamp is permanent and never cleared in production. clear_miracle_cost is defined but has zero call sites (grep finds none outside its own definition). set_miracle_cost stamps card.miracle_cost/has_miracle indefinitely, so once a card is stamped while Lorehold is in play it keeps miracle forever: after Lorehold leaves the battlefield the grant should end, and after the card leaves the controller's hand (cast, discarded, etc.) it should no longer be a controller-hand instant/sorcery. Because draw_card gates only on get_miracle_cost(card) is not None, a previously-stamped card drawn later (e.g. shuffled back, returned to hand then re-drawn) would still offer a miracle cast even though Lorehold is gone. Recommend clearing the stamp when the grant no longer applies (Lorehold leaves play / card leaves hand) or re-deriving the grant dynamically at draw time rather than relying on a sticky attribute.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"engine/casting.py\",\n    \"line\": 332,\n    \"comment\": \"cast_spell_alternative does not apply get_cost_reduction to the alternative cost the way cast_spell does. That is arguably correct for miracle (you pay the alternative cost as printed), but if this function becomes the shared path for other alternative-cost mechanics it may surprise callers expecting cost reduction to apply. Worth a one-line note in the docstring that cost reduction is intentionally not applied to the alternative cost.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"engine/turn.py\",\n    \"line\": 44,\n    \"comment\": \"Resetting cards_drawn_this_turn for all players at the untap step is safe today (grep shows the counter is read only by engine/miracle.py). Note it resets at the ACTIVE player's untap, i.e. once per turn cycle, so a player's counter spans from their own untap through the next player's turns until their following untap. For miracle's 'first card you drew this turn' this is the desired per-turn-rotation reset, but if future mechanics need a strict per-player-turn counter this shared reset point would be wrong. No action needed now; flagging the semantics.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":49727,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":879,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnosti