{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01JonuVc2rogLVPSqUCPBnLa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JMvX5qGdobLCob5RZUuNP3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_226/review.json","content":"[\n  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 165, \"comment\": \"Rules-fidelity gap (accepted): Silverquill itself is excluded from the casualty sacrifice candidates, but rule 702.153 permits sacrificing ANY creature with power >= 1, including Silverquill. I assessed whether a rules-accurate approach (Silverquill eligible) could pass the pinned tests unchanged: it cannot. In test_no_eligible_creature_means_no_copy the board is [Silverquill(4), wall(0)] with script [True]; if Silverquill were eligible the single-eligible auto-select path would sacrifice Silverquill and create a copy, making the test's `len(stack) == before` and `wall not in graveyard`-only assertions fail (a copy would appear). The test's whole premise is that a 0-power wall leaves 'no eligible creature', which only holds if the source is excluded. Since the tests were arbitrated in the TDD phase and cannot be modified, the exclusion is the minimal model that satisfies the contract — flagged advisory, not strict, as instructed. Recommend documenting this as a known fidelity gap for a future fully-rules-accurate casualty generalisation.\"},\n  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 116, \"comment\": \"Stash-on-condition pattern (`source._casualty_spell = spell`) is set inside the trigger condition and read later at resolution. If the controller casts two qualifying instant/sorcery spells before the first casualty trigger resolves (two triggers on the stack), the second condition evaluation overwrites _casualty_spell, so both triggers would copy the most-recent spell. Not exercised by the pinned tests (which resolve one trigger at a time), but it is a latent correctness bug for multi-spell turns. Prefer binding the spell into the effect closure (capture per-registration) rather than a single mutable attribute on the card.\"},\n  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 232, \"comment\": \"_choose_new_targets builds the legal-target set ad hoc (iterating each player's battlefield permanents plus the players themselves and applying req.filter_fn). This omits other target zones (e.g. spells on the stack, cards in graveyard/exile) and ignores any non-filter requirement constraints (target counts, 'up to N', distinctness). For the casualty copy of a typical burn/instant this happens to work, and no pinned test exercises re-targeting, but it diverges from the engine's normal targeting pipeline. Consider routing copy re-targeting through the same target-selection helper cast_spell uses to stay consistent and avoid future divergence.\"}\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":471,"cache_read_input_tokens":48230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":471,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016botgDJ1X78ZG2b9xXKUaf","session_id":"5ed3988e-944a-4908-bc92-4f8937d76445","uuid":"719843f7-a100-4c97-bf8b-8688b2ec6050","request_id":"req_011CbcW4GG79u7NoM5rpcctL","subagent_type":"Reviewer","task_description":"Review sos_226 implementation"}
